WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:21.500
It is impossible to know just how many microbial
species there are in the world.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:28.330
One recent estimate put the number somewhere
around 1 trillion species.

00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:33.760
And the vast majority of those organisms still
have yet to be discovered.

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:39.860
But even with our relatively limited knowledge
of the microcosmos, being able to identify

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:46.730
microbes when we can is important, from helping
doctors diagnose their patients to guiding

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:49.260
scientists through their observations.

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:54.900
And sometimes the goals are a little less
urgent but still meaningful.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:59.940
Like for James, our Master of Microscopes
who collects these organisms and tends to

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:05.890
them, and for the rest of us on the Journey
to the Microcosmos team who want to share

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:07.680
what we've learned.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:13.450
We love showing off these organisms, and the
more specific we can get when we identify

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:18.849
them, the more we can tell you about their
lives.

00:01:18.849 --> 00:01:25.150
But even if we humans have only discovered
a small fraction of all the microbes, there's

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:31.450
still a lot that we have found and named,
so figuring out what exactly is in our samples

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:32.500
can be difficult.

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:39.360
It’s like playing the board game Guess Who?,
but first you have to solve a maze to find

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:40.630
the right board.

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:46.330
And even when you do find it, you realize
you are playing with the largest Guess Who?

00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:53.979
board ever, and still, you can’t be sure
it even holds the answer

00:01:53.979 --> 00:01:59.000
As we'll talk about later, scientists have
devised a number of molecular methods that

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.759
make this task less daunting.

00:02:01.759 --> 00:02:07.950
But we do things the old-fashioned way: we
just look at the organisms.

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:13.290
We collect our samples from nature, peering
in to an assembly of microscopic animals and

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:20.349
single-celled creatures, looking for clues
in their morphology that will hopefully reveal

00:02:20.349 --> 00:02:21.950
what they are.

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:23.540
So come along with us.

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:29.680
Let’s take you through some of the questions
that help guide us through this process.

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:42.360
The best place to start is to ask yourself,
in the broadest sense, what you are looking at.

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:44.320
Is it multicellular?

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:46.760
Is it a unicellular eukaryote?

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:48.730
A prokaryote?

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:53.780
We'll start with the multicellular organisms,
in part because they're generally a little

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:56.060
easier to identify.

00:02:56.060 --> 00:03:02.390
There are fewer of them in total, and their
bodies just have more going on, and that means

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:05.190
that there’s more clues for us to find.

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:09.560
Let's take the tardigrade, as an example,
but pretend that you don't know what tardigrades

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:10.640
look like.

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:15.579
You went down to your favorite pond, gathered
some water, and now you're looking at what

00:03:15.579 --> 00:03:17.370
you collected through the microscope.

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:23.340
On the other side is this delightful, bumbling
bit of life.

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:28.239
You see some stubby legs waving around, a
pair of eyes, and maybe some strange things

00:03:28.239 --> 00:03:30.970
moving around inside its body.

00:03:30.970 --> 00:03:33.280
All those details are helpful.

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:36.140
Legs are a great clue, as are eyes.

00:03:36.140 --> 00:03:41.590
And inside, the moving things you're watching
seem a bit like organs.

00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:46.100
If you weren't sure that you were looking
at a multicellular organism before,

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:47.800
you probably are now.

00:03:52.769 --> 00:03:57.280
And beyond what you see, there’s also what
you know, you know that you got this little

00:03:57.290 --> 00:04:01.170
guy from a pond, meaning a freshwater environment.

00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:05.810
That means you can rule out organisms that
only live in marine environments.

00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:08.430
So what now?

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:13.670
You might be able to get help from a freshwater
biology book, but if you're a hobbyist, those

00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:16.440
books might not be worth the cost.

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:22.610
Luckily, as old-fashioned as our methodology
is, we have a resource that the microbe hunters

00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:29.389
of yore did not: the good ol’ internet,
a source not just of cat pictures and arguments,

00:04:29.389 --> 00:04:36.280
but also of the many microorganisms that scientists
and science enthusiasts like yourself have

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:37.910
studied and documented.

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:44.290
Sometimes, all you need to do is just a simple
search for something like "freshwater microscopic

00:04:44.290 --> 00:04:50.120
animal" and look through images until you
find something that matches what you're looking at.

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:55.520
Now, of course, "freshwater microscopic animal"
can bring up a whole range of species, like

00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:58.780
rotifers, hydras, and gastrotrichs.

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:03.580
But each of those animals is very different
from the others, which can help narrow down

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:08.730
what your unidentified microbial organism
is and is not.

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:13.320
If you find this process overwhelming at first,
because you are trying to narrow down the

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:19.020
vastness of the microcosmos into just one
species, that’s okay!

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:22.300
Every time you do this, you will get better
at it.

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:28.190
It takes years of practice and experience
to figure out how to use all of the information

00:05:28.190 --> 00:05:32.560
available to you and pick out the most useful details.

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:39.000
And like a baby tardigrade waving its legs
for the first time, we all have to start somewhere.

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:44.580
With single-celled eukaryotes, the process
starts to get more difficult.

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:50.720
You won't see the complex structures and systems
that distinguish multicellular organisms,

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:55.230
but you will see organelles that can help
clarify what you're looking at.

00:05:55.230 --> 00:06:01.250
For example, one major clue can be picking
out which organisms have those beating hairs

00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:07.430
called cilia versus which ones have more whip-like
hair called flagella.

00:06:07.430 --> 00:06:12.190
And if you see the green color of chloroplasts,
you know that you need to find an organism

00:06:12.190 --> 00:06:14.180
capable of photosynthesis.

00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:20.620
For example, what if you see the tell-tale
trumpet morphology in your collection?

00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:24.170
“Aha,” you might say, “that is a Stentor!”

00:06:24.170 --> 00:06:28.440
And you would be correct, but which Stentor?

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:34.810
While Stentors have varying numbers of micronuclei,
these are hard to see without staining.

00:06:34.810 --> 00:06:39.500
The macronucleus, however, is visible and
can be helpful.

00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:45.990
For Stentor coeruleus, the macronucleus resembles
beads strung together across the cell which

00:06:45.990 --> 00:06:49.360
is called a moniliform nucleus.

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:55.030
Stentor polymorphous also has a moniliform
nucleus, but it’s easier to distinguish

00:06:55.030 --> 00:07:00.300
from the Stentor coeruleus because of the
endosymbiotic algae that turn it green.

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:07.820
Stentor igneus, by contrast, is smaller, and
has pink pigments, and contains an oval macronucleus.

00:07:07.820 --> 00:07:13.780
Stentors are a relatively easier genus to
pick apart because there are only about 20

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:16.740
species of Stentors to decide between.

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:22.170
Other genera can have hundreds of species,
and the differences might be more subtle.That’s

00:07:22.170 --> 00:07:28.490
why sometimes you’ll see our organisms labeled
by the most narrow description we feel comfortable

00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:34.860
giving, whether that’s identifying an organism
solely by its genus, or even going more broad

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:37.610
and just calling it a “ciliate.”

00:07:37.610 --> 00:07:42.660
These are cases where we just don’t know
enough...yet.

00:07:42.660 --> 00:07:48.910
For those of you who remember our mystery,
real time video, it took James a year to be

00:07:48.910 --> 00:07:52.070
sure of what organisms he was watching.

00:07:52.070 --> 00:07:58.100
And until he identified the mystery organism, we didn’t know what was happening in that video.

00:07:58.100 --> 00:08:02.060
And of course it’s even more challenging
to identify bacteria.

00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:07.170
We can use morphology to parse out cyanobacteria
and sulfur bacteria.

00:08:07.170 --> 00:08:12.020
But with most of the bacteria in our samples,
it's just impossible to tell without genetic

00:08:12.020 --> 00:08:17.760
sequencing, a technique that is not in our
toolkit at the moment.

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:19.600
And for us, that's okay.

00:08:22.300 --> 00:08:27.740
But there are cases where it's important to
dig further, and scientists have developed

00:08:27.750 --> 00:08:32.770
many molecular techniques to identify the
microbes in their samples.

00:08:32.770 --> 00:08:39.140
There are stains to emphasize certain identifying
structures, and biochemical tests to reveal

00:08:39.140 --> 00:08:41.320
the presence of specific enzymes.

00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:47.110
Also, of course, there’s gene sequencing
and other more advanced chemical methods to

00:08:47.110 --> 00:08:50.579
probe what species a sample contains.

00:08:50.579 --> 00:08:55.959
For James, who does the work of identifying
our microbes without these molecular tests,

00:08:55.959 --> 00:09:02.620
what he's able to name comes from years of studying and memorizing the drawings and pictures

00:09:02.620 --> 00:09:10.329
he’s found in various books and keys, as
well an ongoing habit of reading papers every day.

00:09:10.329 --> 00:09:16.940
It's more a way of life, a habit borne out
of science, an art, a labor of love.

00:09:19.580 --> 00:09:24.920
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:09:24.920 --> 00:09:29.540
And all of these people on the screen right
now, these are our patrons on Patreon.

00:09:29.540 --> 00:09:33.990
We only have one Patreon tier, it’s $8 a
month.

00:09:33.990 --> 00:09:38.819
And with it, you get your name featured in
the end of our video here, a weekly hi-resolution

00:09:38.819 --> 00:09:44.620
image of one of our micro friends, and an
extended video featuring uncut footage with

00:09:44.620 --> 00:09:49.370
no narration from me, just Andrew Huang’s
wonderful music.

00:09:49.370 --> 00:09:55.480
And if you want to join up and help us out,
you can do that at patreon.com/journeytomicro.

00:09:55.480 --> 00:10:01.790
If you want to see more from our Master of Microscopes James, check out Jam &amp; Germs on Instagram.

00:10:01.790 --> 00:10:07.060
And if you want to see more from us, just
go to youtube.com/microcosmos.

